<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Feb 20, 2023">
<CPscript title="Branching the Soccer Ball" level="min" screenmode="map" >
<description>A 32-circle packing with dodecahedral symmetry mimics the familiar 'soccer ball' combinatorics. Here we display it and its branched version as shown in Figure 6.11 of "Explorations in Complex Analysis". The challenge: how can we compute the radii in p1 (CirclePack can't do it)?</description>
<text> The map from p0 to p1 is a discrete rational map of degree 7. So the range sphere is covered by 7 sheets. There are 12 circles of degree 5 which are branch points (color coded) and 20 circles of degree 6 (white). </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" iconname="klines.png">act 0;Cleanse;infile_read soccer32.p;color -c 100 a;color -c s0 a(1 12);Disp -w -cf -f;act 1;infile_read branched32.p;set_sv -d;color -c 100 a;color -c s0 a(1 12);Disp -w -c a(13 32) -cf a(1 12);Map 0 1; </cmd>
<text> This is a second discrete analogue of the classical function F(z)=z^2(z^5-7)/(7z^5+1); compare to the 42-circle version in "Rational.cps". 

Pressing 'b' on branch point in the domain will display its flower in p0 and its image flower in p1. The keys 'c' and 'f' toggle the display mode between circles and dual faces. </text>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="f" mnemonic="f" tooltip="display face mode" iconname="centrejust.png">Disp -p0 -w -dff;Disp -p1 -w -df -dff a(1 12) </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="c" mnemonic="c" tooltip="display circle mode" iconname="dnd_multi.png">Disp -p0 -w -c -cf a(1 12);Disp -p1 -w -c -cf a(1 12); </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="b" mnemonic="b" tooltip="display a branch point's flower" iconname="psi.png">set_Vlist z #XY;set_Flist Iv Vlist[0];disp -p0 -w -ffc150 Flist -cf Vlist[0] -c Iv Vlist[0];disp -p1 -w -ffc150 Flist -cf Vlist[0] -c Iv Vlist[0] </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
<CPdata>
    <AboutImage name="AboutImage.jpg">

    </AboutImage>
